22:52:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I totally get why you’d think that—when every operation you try on a bigger array takes longer, it feels like arrays just scale linearly. But it depends entirely on **which operation** you’re doing. ### 1. Array indexing (e.g., `arr[5000]`) is truly O(1), always An array stores its elements in a single contiguous block of memory. When you write `arr[i]`, the computer calculates the memory address directly: ``` address = base_address + (i * size_of_each_element) ``` That’s a multiply and an add, which take the same amount of time whether the array has 10 elements or 10 million. The CPU doesn’t scan anything; it just jumps to the right spot. If you measured the time to access **one specific index** (like the 1,000th element), you’d see it stays nearly constant no matter how large the array grows. ### 2. Why you observe “bigger array = slower” It’s almost certainly because the operations you’re doing are **not** simple index lookups. Common culprits: - **Looping over the whole array** (`for item in arr`, `sum(arr)`, `print all elements`) – This is O(n) because every element gets visited. A bigger array means more visits, so the total time goes up linearly. - **Searching by value** (`arr.index(value)`, `value in arr`) – The system has no idea where the value lives, so it must scan from the beginning until it finds a match. That’s O(n) in the worst case. - **Inserting/removing in the middle** – Shifting elements is O(n). So when you say “the bigger the array, the slower it runs,” you’re almost certainly performing an O(n) operation on that array. If you were only doing random index accesses like `x = arr[random_index]` repeated many times, you’d see that time per access is independent of array size (apart from caching effects, which can even make large arrays *faster* due to memory locality). ### 3. Quick mental test Imagine an array `a` with 1,000,000 elements, and another `b` with 10 elements. Run this pseudo-code: ``` start = now() do 1,000,000 times: x = a[50000] // always same index print elapsed time start = now() do 1,000,000 times: x = b[1] // same index print elapsed time ``` The times will be roughly equal, showing that indexing is O(1). ### 4. How this ties to hash tables A hash table uses a **hash function** to convert a *key* (e.g., a string) into an integer index, and then uses that index to jump to a bucket inside an internal array. That jump is O(1) just like array indexing. Then it might check one or two items in the bucket (hopefully O(1) average). So key lookup in a hash table is essentially “compute hash → array index → directly access that spot”. But if you try to *search a hash table by value* (e.g., “which key has value 42?”), you’re out of luck—the hash table only indexes keys, so you’d have to scan all entries, which is O(n) just like searching an array by value. **In summary:** - Array access by index → O(1) (constant time, regardless of size). - Array operation that touches all elements or searches by value → O(n) (scales with size). - Your observation of slowdown is completely valid, but it’s because the operations you’re benchmarking are O(n), not because indexing slows down. 😊
