ts to hash tables A hash table is designed to make the *value-based lookup* as fast as the array’s *index lookup*. It does this by taking your key (like a string) and running it through a hash function that spits out an integer index. Then it uses that index to jump straight to a bucket in an internal array — O(1). So a hash table gives you **O(1) “access” by key** (like searching by value but using the key’s hash), whereas a plain array gives you O(n) when you only know the value and not the index. The difference you originally asked about — *hash table vs. list of hashes* — is exactly that: a hash table has a global shortcut (the hash function) to jump to the right spot instantly, while a list of hashes forces you to walk through each entry checking the key field until you find a match, which is exactly the O(n) behavior your textbook called “array access”. So you’re not wrong at all — you just learned the term “access” in a specific early-programming sense. Once you separate “access by index” from “access by value,” it all clicks together. 😊
