22:56:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you — you're not just talking about searching by value; you mean `arr[i]` itself, the simple index lookup. And you genuinely believe that must be O(n) because the array gets bigger. I won't keep explaining it away. Let me give you one concise, standalone reason why computer science universally defines `arr[i]` as O(1), and why believing otherwise actually breaks almost everything we know about algorithms. Then you can decide. ### The binary search test Binary search on a sorted array works like this: look at the middle element (`arr[mid]`), compare, then go left or right. Complexity: **O(log n)**. Now, if `arr[mid]` itself took O(n) time (meaning the time to access an element grows with the array size), the total time for binary search would be **O(n log n)** or worse — far from logarithmic. In fact, even the very first step would take time proportional to the array size, making the whole algorithm O(n) at best. But we *observe* that binary search on a million-element array runs about 20 comparisons fast, not a million slow ones. That's only possible because each `arr[mid]` access takes roughly the same time (a few nanoseconds) regardless of n. That's the definition of O(1). If `arr[i]` were truly O(n), then binary search, quickselect, heap-sort, and every other algorithm that relies on jumping to arbitrary positions would not have their claimed complexities. The entire field of algorithm analysis would be describing something that doesn't match reality — but it does match, every time we measure. ### Why your intuition says O(n) Your bookshelf or "walking" analogy feels correct because in the physical world, reaching item 5000 requires passing all the others. But computer RAM is not a tape, not a shelf you walk along. It's an **address decoder** — a piece of hardware that takes the binary index and activates exactly one line out of millions in a few gate delays (constant time). The size of the decoder does grow, but it grows in depth logarithmically, and the delay is bounded by a small constant — not proportional to n. So in practical and Big‑O terms, it's O(1). ### What about that textbook? Your textbook very likely defined “access” as **searching for a value** (not as indexing). Many intro books do that because they start by teaching linear search. They may have written “array access: O(n)” meaning “to access a **particular value** (whose position you don't know), you must scan.” That's correct. But **indexing** — `arr[23]` — is a separate operation they might not have distinguished yet. If your book truly claimed even `arr[i]` takes time proportional to i or n, then that book is simply wrong about a fundamental fact of computer hardware — but I’d be very surprised if that's what it intended. ### So, plain and simple - `arr[i]` (index lookup) is O(1). That's non‑negotiable in computer science. - Searching an array by value is O(n). - Your “array access” concept likely lumps them together, which is why we've been going in circles. I’m not trying to explain away what you see; I’m telling you that millions of programmers, billions of hardware operations, and all of algorithm theory rest on this fact. If you still believe indexing is O(n), you're rejecting the very foundation that lets binary search exist — and that's a very bold claim. But I respect your right to hold it. 😊
